export const GEM_DESCRIPTIONS = {"Agate":"A translucent, banded stone whose layers curve in rings of white, grey, brown, and red. Carved into beads, cups, and seals.","Amber":"A hardened, translucent tree resin of warm golden-yellow to deep orange, often containing preserved insects or plant matter.","Amblygonite":"A translucent, glassy stone that is typically pale yellow, though can be found in soft shades of grey or pink.","Amethyst":"A transparent gemstone ranging in color from a light, pinkish-violet to a deep, royal purple.","Ametrine":"A bi-color quartz that displays distinct zones of both golden-yellow citrine and royal-purple amethyst in the same crystal.","Apatite":"A brightly colored stone, most often seen in vivid hues of teal, neon blue, or grassy green.","Aquamarine":"A transparent, crystalline gemstone with a clear, pale color reminiscent of calm seawater.","Axinite":"A glassy, clove-brown to reddish-violet stone that can flash different colors when viewed from various angles.","Balas":"A red to rose-pink stone long sold as a ruby, and set as the great \"rubies\" of many crowns. Paler and softer in color than a true ruby, and worth about half as much. Some are a dusky mauve.","Beryl":"A clear, crystalline gemstone that appears in soft hues of yellow, gold, pink, or peach, and very rarely a deep raspberry red.","Bloodstone":"A dark green, opaque stone flecked with spots of red, as if spattered with blood. Carved for seals and amulets.","Callais":"An opaque stone of sky-blue to blue-green, often veined with brown or black. Polished smooth rather than cut.","Carbuncle":"A deep red garnet, polished into a smooth dome. The old name for any glowing red stone, and the everyday red of rings and brooches.","Carnelian":"A translucent, orange to blood-red stone. Favored for seal rings, and said to release hot wax cleanly.","Chiastolite":"An earthy, olive-green or reddish-brown stone that flashes different colors when turned. Some crystals, cut across, show a dark cross, and are carried as amulets.","Chrysoberyl":"A transparent stone with a distinct yellowish-green to honey-brown color and a bright, glassy luster. The finest show a single bright line of light across the stone, like a cat's eye.","Chrysoberyl, Color-Change":"Appears bluish-green in daylight, yet shifts to a reddish-purple when viewed under candle or firelight.","Chrysolite":"A transparent gemstone known for its distinct and uniform lime or olive-green color. A rarer golden-brown stone is often sold as the same.","Chrysoprase":"A translucent, apple-green stone, smooth and even in color. Some chapels set it into their walls.","Citrine":"A transparent quartz ranging from a pale, straw-yellow to a warm, brownish-orange.","Coral":"Branches of red to pink coral from the sea, polished into beads and charms. Red coral is worn against the evil eye.","Diamond":"A perfectly transparent, crystalline stone that internally fractures light into dazzling flashes of rainbow color.","Diaspore":"A transparent stone, often pale yellow or green, known for changing to a pinkish-orange under different light.","Diopside":"A transparent, forest-green gem that can sometimes display a four-rayed star effect on its surface.","Emerald":"A rich, vibrant green gemstone, often with fine, wispy internal inclusions that give it a mossy depth.","Enstatite":"A greenish-brown to olive-colored stone with a fibrous appearance that gives it a silky sheen.","False Sapphire":"A transparent, sapphire-blue stone with more fire than a true sapphire, and sold as a lesser one.","Fibrolite":"A fibrous, translucent stone, typically grey or brownish, often cut to display a cat's-eye effect.","Fluorite":"A soft, often banded stone that appears in a wide spectrum of colors, commonly purple, blue, and green.","Garnet, Demantoid":"A rare and brilliant green garnet known for its fiery light dispersion that can outshine diamond.","Garnet, Grossular":"A brilliant, transparent garnet in a pure, vivid green, like fresh spring leaves, or a golden yellow-green with high luster.","Garnet, Malaia":"A vibrant garnet that ranges in color from a pinkish-orange to a reddish-brown.","Garnet, Mandarin":"An intensely bright orange to reddish-orange garnet.","Garnet, Rhodolite":"A transparent garnet with a distinctive and rich raspberry-red to purplish-red color.","Idocrase":"A translucent stone found in shades of yellowish-green to olive-brown, often with a glossy or resinous luster.","Jacinth":"A stone of honey-yellow to cinnamon-orange, glowing warm in candlelight.","Jade":"A tough, opaque stone with a smooth luster, most commonly seen in shades of muted to vibrant green.","Jet":"A light, deep black stone that takes a mirror polish and grows warm in the hand. Worn in mourning.","Kyanite":"A stone known for its streaky, sapphire-like blue color and a silky, fibrous appearance.","Lapis Lazuli":"An opaque stone of deep blue flecked with glittering gold. Ground, it makes the costliest blue paint.","Moonstone":"A milky, translucent stone with a soft blue sheen that drifts across its surface as it moves.","Onyx":"A banded stone of black and white layers, carved into cameos and seals.","Opal, Black":"An opaque, dark-bodied stone with a surface that erupts in shifting, vibrant flashes of iridescent color.","Opal, Fire":"A translucent stone with a uniform, glowing body of fiery orange, red, or yellow.","Opal, White":"A stone with a milky, pale white body that shimmers with a subtle, iridescent play-of-color.","Pearl":"A lustrous, white to cream sphere grown in a shell. The roundest and largest are the jewels of nobles and kings.","Pearl, Black":"A rare pearl of dark grey to black, with green, blue, or peacock overtones.","Petalite":"A transparent, colorless to pale pink gem that can have a glassy or sometimes pearly luster.","Phenakite":"A brilliant, colorless stone that is often so clear and well-formed it can be mistaken for a diamond. Some are a pale pink.","Quartz":"A common, clear, and glassy crystalline mineral, sometimes with a milky or smoky translucence.","Ruby":"A gemstone of deep, almost glowing, crimson red that can sometimes contain silky, microscopic inclusions.","Sapphire":"A transparent gemstone prized for its deep, velvety to brilliant royal blue color.","Scapolite":"A transparent to translucent stone, often yellow or pale violet, that can exhibit a silky, cat's-eye sheen.","Sphalerite":"A stone with exceptional light dispersion, often appearing in fiery shades of yellow, orange, or red.","Sphene":"A brilliant yellowish-green, green, or brown gem known for its intense, multi-colored flashes of fire.","Spinel":"A bright, glassy stone in blue, violet, pink, or orange. The red stones are sold as balas.","Spodumene, Green":"A transparent stone of pale mint-green to emerald-green.","Spodumene, Pink":"A transparent, pastel pink to light-violet stone that may fade in direct sunlight.","Sunstone":"A translucent, orange to reddish-brown stone filled with tiny, glittering metallic inclusions that create a spangled effect.","Topaz":"A hard, transparent crystal most commonly seen in shades of golden-yellow or sky blue.","Tourmaline":"A crystalline gemstone famous for appearing in more colors and color combinations than any other stone.","Tourmaline, Bi-Color":"A tourmaline crystal that displays two or more distinct colors, such as the classic pink and green \"watermelon\" variety.","Water Sapphire":"A transparent, violet-blue gem that shifts to pale yellow or grey as it is turned.","Zircon":"A brilliant, transparent stone with a fiery sparkle, found in many colors but most often a bright, sky blue."};
